{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 32, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "He woke to the sound of birds chirping in perfect, looping cadence—the kind that never varied, never faltered, never grew tired. The sunlight streaming through his window was too golden, too crisp, as if rendered by an overzealous shader. His alarm clock blinked 7:00 AM, but the date was wrong—March 14th, 2047, when he was certain it was October 3rd, 2023. He reached for his coffee mug, and it didn’t clink against the counter; it emitted a soft chime, like a quest notification. Outside, his neighbor waved, but his arm moved in a stiff, three-frame animation, and when he smiled, his teeth glinted with an unnatural shine. A notification hovered at the corner of his vision: “Daily Quest: Morning Routine — Complete? Y/N.” He whispered, “No,” and the world flickered—just once—like a buffer lagging behind reality.\n\n***\n\nBy noon, he’d tested everything: jumping off his roof didn’t kill him—it triggered a slow-fall animation with a cheerful “Safety Net Activated!” message. He tried to call his sister, but her voice mail played a canned recording: “Sorry, NPC unavailable. Please try again later.” He found a hidden alley behind the grocery store, and there, etched into the brick, was a faint, glowing code: // PLAYER_ID: JAMES_K_777 // STATUS: AWAKE // REALITY_OVERRIDE: PENDING. His breath caught. He wasn’t trapped—he was discovered. And somewhere, in a dim room across dimensions, a player had just clicked “Resume Game.” The sky above him darkened, not with clouds, but with the slow, creeping overlay of a loading screen. He closed his eyes and whispered, “I’m not a character.” The game replied, in a voice both mechanical and kind: “Then why are you still playing?”", "logprobs": [{"token": "He", "logprob": -0.6198107600212097}, {"token": " woke", "logprob": -0.060740288347005844}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.020681682974100113}, {"token": " the", "lo